// 1.25cqw baseline grid. A running header (section, workshop title,
// slide number) sits above a full-width rule. The title is in a fixed
// position below it. The lower register holds a lead sentence in the
// left columns and numbered points in the right six. Screenshot slides
// keep the header and title and put the image in the right six columns.
// Numbers, prices, and commands are in the speaker notes.
import Fig from '@/components/slides/Fig.astro'
import Slide from '@/components/slides/Slide.astro'
import SlidesLayout from '@/layouts/SlidesLayout.astro'

import endRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/end-runebender.jpg'
import grid from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-grid.png'
import nodes from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nodes.png'
import nufoEditing from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nufo-editing.png'
import outline from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-outline.png'
import titleDgxSpark from '@/assets/slides/localai/title-dgx-spark.jpg'

const TALK = 'Local AI for Type Design'
---

<SlidesLayout
  title="Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor (slides)"
  description="Workshop slides for Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor, ATypI 2026 Sharjah."
  running="Local AI for Type Design"
  author="Eli Heuer"
>
  <!-- ── Opening ── -->

  <Slide label="Title">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={titleDgxSpark.src}
      alt="Two NVIDIA DGX Spark units stacked on a desk, linked by two QSFP cables, with a keyboard behind them."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <h1 class="t-display descends">Local AI for<br />Type Design</h1>
      <p class="t-head">with the Runebender Font Editor</p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-1-6 over-bleed at-bottom">
      <p class="t-body">Eli Heuer · ATypI 2026 Sharjah</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Runebender is an open-source, cross-platform font editor written in Rust. It runs, trains, and fine-tunes models on your own machine.</p>
      <p>Setup instructions are at runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html. The exercises are in the runebender-local-ai-demo repository.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── 01 Introduction ── -->

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Format">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">01 Introduction</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Format</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">A short demo, then hands-on setup on your own machine.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Demo: training, fine-tuning, open models, nodes</li>
          <li>Setup: a DMG, Cargo, or a coding agent</li>
          <li>Exercises: five folders in the demo repository</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Bring a Mac with Apple silicon or a Linux PC. Windows desktop support is not ready.</p>
      <p>Setup routes: a DMG on a Mac, Cargo on Mac and Linux, or a coding agent pointed at runebender.org.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Questions">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">01 Introduction</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Open questions</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Who controls the tools, the data, and the models?</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Who owns a model trained on a designer’s drawings?</li>
          <li>Can a font license allow or forbid use as training data?</li>
          <li>Should unreleased client fonts go to a cloud service?</li>
          <li>Which glyphs should a model draft, and which must a person draw?</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>These questions frame the workshop. Running models locally answers some of them directly: the drawings, the training data, and the model files stay on the designer’s machine.</p>
      <p>Custom type for clients is often under a non-disclosure agreement. Sending unreleased glyphs to a cloud model can break that agreement. A local model does not send anything.</p>
      <p>Most font licenses were written before model training. The SIL Open Font License does not mention it. Commercial EULAs differ by foundry.</p>
      <p>The structure follows Nathan Lambert’s lecture “Open Language Models” (Harvard, October 2026), which opens with three questions about who should control the technology.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── 02 Open models ── -->

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Why local AI">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Why local AI</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">One AI company serving every studio would control the type industry.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Independence from any one company</li>
          <li>Drawings, models, and fonts stay yours</li>
          <li>Client work stays on your machine</li>
          <li>No fee for each use</li>
          <li>Models you can open and study</li>
          <li>A model on disk keeps working</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The core reason is concentration of power. A company that runs the models for every studio sees every studio’s drawings and requests, sets the prices, can change the terms or close access, and can release its own fonts and font tools. With local models, no single company stands between the type industry and its own tools.</p>
      <p>Ownership: a foundry owns its fonts; with local models it also owns the models trained on them. Lambert’s example from another industry: in August 2026, Harvey post-trained an open Kimi K3 base model so law firms can own their models.</p>
      <p>Confidentiality: custom type for clients is often under a non-disclosure agreement. A local model sends nothing.</p>
      <p>Cost: no per-token fee. Understanding: a model that can be opened, trained, and studied, which is how a field learns how a technology works.</p>
      <p>Durability: a model file on disk runs as long as the hardware does. A cloud model can be retired, repriced, or changed by its provider. Mark Zuckerberg’s list of what developers want from open models covers the same ground: train, fine-tune, and distill their own models; “control our own destiny and not get locked into a closed vendor”; protect their data; run at lower cost; and build on the ecosystem that will last. (Open Source AI is the Path Forward, July 2024.)</p>
      <p>Lambert’s general list of reasons people want open models: commoditizing complements, scientific progress, preventing concentration of power, and freedom. (Open Language Models, Harvard, October 2026.)</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Protecting know-how">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Protecting know-how</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">A foundry that works through an AI lab teaches that lab how fonts are made.</p>
          <p class="t-credit">Alex Karp, G20 Innovation Ministerial, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Your process<span class="val">sent to the lab</span></li>
          <li>Consumer chats<span class="val">used, by default</span></li>
          <li>Business plans<span class="val">not used, for now</span></li>
          <li>Labs enter markets<span class="val">Claude Design</span></li>
          <li>Firms train their own<span class="val">Harvey, 2026</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Alex Karp, Palantir, at the G20 Innovation Ministerial (reported September 2026): companies that deploy AI without the right architecture are “exfiltrating, not intentionally, your alpha to the company that provides the model.” And: “This isn’t sovereignty. This is anti-sovereignty.” Alpha is his word for a company’s proprietary know-how. Palantir sells the layer he proposes, so he has a commercial interest.</p>
      <p>For a type studio, the alpha is the process: how it draws, spaces, kerns, and corrects, and the judgment in every correction. A studio that does this work through a frontier lab’s chat or agent sends that process to the lab. If every small foundry did so, the lab would hold the industry’s combined method, and could release its own fonts.</p>
      <p>Precedent: in July 2026, David Sacks said Anthropic “blindsided” Figma by launching Claude Design, a competing design tool, while an Anthropic executive had served on Figma’s board. That is Sacks’s account.</p>
      <p>Terms, to be fair: since September 2025, Anthropic trains on consumer chats (Free, Pro, Max) unless the user opts out. Commercial and API use is not used for training under current terms. Training is not the only route for know-how to leave: a lab sees what its customers work on, and terms can change.</p>
      <p>The alternative is ownership. In August 2026, Harvey post-trained an open Kimi K3 base model so law firms can own their models. Mercor and SkyRL post-trained an open Qwen3.5 model on expert data and released the weights. A foundry can do the same with local models trained on its own drawings.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Open formats">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Open formats</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">Type has seen this before: closed formats gave way to open ones.</p>
          <p class="t-credit">Zuckerberg, Open Source AI is the Path Forward, 2024</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>PostScript Type 1<span class="val">support ended 2023</span></li>
          <li>OpenType<span class="val">an ISO standard</span></li>
          <li>UFO<span class="val">open source format</span></li>
          <li>Unix to Linux<span class="val">the same pattern</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Zuckerberg’s argument starts with Unix: each large company built its own closed version, and open source Linux replaced them, first because it was cheaper and could be modified, later because it was better and had a larger ecosystem. He expects AI to follow the same path.</p>
      <p>Type went through the same change. PostScript Type 1 was Adobe’s closed format; Adobe ended Type 1 support in new releases of its apps in January 2023. OpenType, an open specification from Adobe and Microsoft, is published as an ISO standard (Open Font Format). UFO is an open source format that most font editors read and write, and Runebender uses it.</p>
      <p>The same choice is coming for the models in type design tools: closed models behind an API, or open models in a format anyone can run.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Open weights and open source">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">What is open?</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-half stack">
          <h3 class="t-head">Open weights</h3>
          <p class="t-body">Released to download and run. Like a font binary.</p>
          <ol class="sw-points">
            <li>Alibaba<span class="val">Qwen</span></li>
            <li>Google<span class="val">Gemma</span></li>
            <li>OpenAI<span class="val">gpt-oss</span></li>
            <li>DeepSeek<span class="val">V3, R1</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="sw-half sw-half-b stack">
          <h3 class="t-head">Open source</h3>
          <p class="t-body">Weights, data, and code. Like font sources.</p>
          <ol class="sw-points">
            <li>EleutherAI<span class="val">Pythia</span></li>
            <li>Ai2<span class="val">OLMo, Tülu</span></li>
            <li>Hugging Face<span class="val">SmolLM</span></li>
            <li>Marin<span class="val">Marin</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="t-credit">After Nathan Lambert, Open Language Models, Harvard, October 2026</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Open weights are like a compiled font: you can use it, but you cannot see how it was made. Open source is like a font released with its UFO or Glyphs sources and build scripts: you can study it, change it, and rebuild it.</p>
      <p>Both differ from Claude and GPT, which are available only through an API. Openness is a spectrum (Solaiman, 2023): NVIDIA Nemotron releases much of its training data, but not all.</p>
      <p>Moonshot AI (Kimi) and Zhipu (GLM) are also leading open-weight labs. Meta’s Llama and Muse models are open weights under custom licenses. Marin is from the Marin community and Open Athena.</p>
      <p>The Runebender font models are open in the full sense: the training data is the designer’s own font sources, and font-ml is open source.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="How close open models are">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">How close open models are</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">The best open models trail the best closed models by a few months.</p>
          <p class="t-credit">Artificial Analysis and Epoch AI, via Nathan Lambert, October 2026</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>General intelligence index<span class="val">about 3 months</span></li>
          <li>Software engineering<span class="val">about 5 months</span></li>
          <li>The gap is getting smaller</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The leading open models in October 2026 come from Chinese labs: Xiaomi MiMo-V2.6-Pro, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, GLM-5.3. American open models trail by another 3 to 4 months.</p>
      <p>These leading models have about 1 trillion parameters and do not run on a laptop. The models in this workshop are much smaller. The point is that open weights are not a second-rate category.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Distillation">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Distillation</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">Training a model on the output of another model.</p>
          <p class="t-credit">After Nathan Lambert, Open Language Models, Harvard, October 2026</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Teacher and student models<span class="val">Hinton, 2015</span></li>
          <li>Training on any model’s output</li>
          <li>Extracting output from a closed API</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Three meanings. The first is the original technique: a student model learns the full output distribution of a teacher. The second is post-training on outputs of any model, which covers most synthetic data. The third, the current debate, is taking hidden reasoning traces from closed APIs; Lambert calls it adversarial distillation.</p>
      <p>Nasta’liq Distilled is the first meaning: a neural font trained on the shaped output of Gulzar, an OFL font.</p>
      <p>The font-industry version of the debate is training on other foundries’ fonts. The Runebender models train only on the designer’s own drawings.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Google Fonts as training data">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Google Fonts</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">Most fonts were never built as training data.</p>
          <p class="t-credit">From elih.net/blog/virtua-grotesk</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Points land anywhere, not on a grid</li>
          <li>Each family has its own conventions</li>
          <li>Normalizing them removes information</li>
          <li>Fonts made from them stay under the OFL</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Many font AI projects train on scraped fonts and Google Fonts, which differ in every dimension that matters. The coordinates land wherever each designer put them, and the contours are as individual as handwriting.</p>
      <p>A model trained on that mix spends its capacity reconciling different drawing habits instead of learning one design. Preprocessing to normalize or quantize the fonts removes information, and mixed sources add variation unrelated to the target design.</p>
      <p>Simon Cozens notes that “vectorization of glyph images has been historically very bad,” and his own experiment with Google Fonts “completely failed.” (State of AI font generation, June 2026.)</p>
      <p>Licensing: the Google Fonts catalog is mostly OFL. Under OFL-FAQ 1.25, a font produced by a model trained on OFL sources is a derivative and must stay under the OFL.</p>
      <p>Virtua-12M-v0.1 was pretrained on 28,770 Regular and Bold pairs traced from Google Fonts, then fine-tuned on Virtua Grotesk. The corrected corpus drops the Google Fonts stage and trains on Virtua Grotesk alone.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="A better open library">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">A better open library</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">With AI, an open font library can meet one high standard.</p>
          <p class="t-credit">After Bill Gurley, Open Source Strategy, May 2026</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Google Fonts made web fonts free</li>
          <li>Its quality and conventions vary</li>
          <li>Local models draft, designers grade</li>
          <li>Every font is also clean training data</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Gurley describes open source as a corporate strategy: companies use it “to neutralize a stronger competitor, commoditize an expensive input, align an industry around a shared standard, or head off a regulatory crisis.” His examples include Android against the iPhone, Kubernetes against AWS, RISC-V against per-unit chip licensing, and Overture Maps against Google’s map data.</p>
      <p>Gurley does not discuss fonts. My reading: Google Fonts fits his pattern. Free, open fonts commoditized an input to the web, Docs, and Android, which are Google’s businesses. It succeeded, and the fonts are free and widely used, but the families were drawn by many designers to many standards, and quality varies.</p>
      <p>AI changes the cost. A small team can draw a core set by hand, let local models draft the rest in the same conventions, and grade every glyph. The result can be an open library held to one standard, where every font is also usable training data, because it was drawn as a system from the start. Virtua Grotesk is the first test of that approach.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Licenses">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Licenses</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-half stack">
          <h3 class="t-head">Models</h3>
          <p class="t-body">The models Runebender uses are permissive.</p>
          <ol class="sw-points">
            <li>Qwen3.5 4B<span class="val">Apache 2.0</span></li>
            <li>Qwen3.6 27B, 35B A3B<span class="val">Apache 2.0</span></li>
            <li>Gemma 4 26B A4B<span class="val">Apache 2.0</span></li>
            <li>gpt-oss 20B<span class="val">Apache 2.0</span></li>
            <li>Whisper small<span class="val">MIT</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="sw-half sw-half-b stack">
          <h3 class="t-head">Software</h3>
          <p class="t-body">The programs that run them are permissive too.</p>
          <ol class="sw-points">
            <li>llama.cpp<span class="val">MIT</span></li>
            <li>whisper.cpp<span class="val">MIT</span></li>
            <li>Runebender, font-ml<span class="val">Apache 2.0 or MIT</span></li>
            <li>Models trained on your fonts<span class="val">not defined</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="t-credit">From the model files and project licenses, October 2026</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Permissive licenses (MIT, Apache 2.0) allow use, modification, sale, and redistribution, as long as the notice is kept. They work like the SIL Open Font License, without its Reserved Font Name clause.</p>
      <p>Custom model licenses add conditions and are not open-source licenses: revenue limits (Kimi K3 and Muse Spark Open above $20M for inference services), branding (“Built with Llama”, like a Reserved Font Name), and acceptable-use policies (Llama, Gemma 1 to 3). Gemma 4 moved to Apache 2.0. Most of these terms have not been tested in court. (Nathan Lambert, Open Language Models, Harvard, October 2026.)</p>
      <p>The chat models are 4-bit GGUF conversions published by Unsloth. The conversion keeps the original license.</p>
      <p>No license says what a model trained on a font inherits from the font. A model trained on an OFL font, such as the Virtua Grotesk models, is an open question. The Shapes font in the demo repository is Apache 2.0 or MIT.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Training on fonts">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Training on fonts</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">A model trained on an OFL font: what carries over?</p>
          <p class="t-credit">SIL OFL-FAQ 1.25 and 1.26, November 2023</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Fonts it drafts<span class="val">derivative, stays OFL</span></li>
          <li>Graphics it makes<span class="val">normal use</span></li>
          <li>The model weights<span class="val">not addressed</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>FAQ 1.25: “Any font produced from such systems whose input or training data contains any source file from a font project licensed under the OFL should be considered a derivative work.” It must remain under the OFL. To release it under another license, ask the authors for permission.</p>
      <p>FAQ 1.26: OFL fonts can be used by AI-based design tools “as long as the resulting work is not intended to be a font.” Graphical output is “similar to normal usage.”</p>
      <p>Neither the license nor the FAQ says whether the trained weights are themselves Font Software or a Modified Version. That is the open question.</p>
      <p>The FAQ is SIL’s interpretation, not a court ruling.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Font licenses and AI">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Font licenses</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-half stack">
          <h3 class="t-head">SIL Open Font License</h3>
          <p class="t-body">Font output stays under the OFL.</p>
          <ol class="sw-points">
            <li>Condition 5<span class="val">OFL only</span></li>
            <li>Modified Version<span class="val">includes new formats</span></li>
            <li>FAQ 1.25<span class="val">model fonts derivative</span></li>
            <li>FAQ 1.26<span class="val">graphics are normal use</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="sw-half sw-half-b stack">
          <h3 class="t-head">Foundry licenses</h3>
          <p class="t-body">Many now forbid AI training.</p>
          <ol class="sw-points">
            <li>Monotype<span class="val">no training on glyphs</span></li>
            <li>Commercial Type<span class="val">no AI use</span></li>
            <li>Ama, BAL<span class="val">no training data</span></li>
            <li>Older licenses<span class="val">silent</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="t-credit">License texts and EULAs as published, October 2026</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>OFL condition 5: “The Font Software, modified or unmodified, in part or in whole, must be distributed entirely under this license, and must not be distributed under any other license.” A Modified Version is “any derivative made by adding to, deleting, or substituting, in part or in whole, any of the components of the Original Version, by changing formats or by porting the Font Software to a new environment.” Documents created with the font are exempt.</p>
      <p>Monotype’s MyFonts EULAs forbid using the font software, rendered glyphs, outlines, or kerning data to develop, train, or fine-tune AI models; metadata such as names and classification tags is allowed. Ama Foundry’s clause also covers output that mimics the design. BAL Foundry forbids dataset inclusion and automated analysis. Commercial Type forbids use with AI services and AI programming.</p>
      <p>Most EULAs were written before AI design tools. Check each foundry’s current text; these clauses change.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Copyright">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Copyright</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <div class="sw-lead stack">
          <p class="t-head">In the US, the font file is protected. The letterforms are not.</p>
          <p class="t-credit">Adobe v. Southern Software, 1998</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Font software<span class="val">copyright</span></li>
          <li>Typeface design<span class="val">not copyrightable</span></li>
          <li>Novel designs<span class="val">design patent</span></li>
          <li>Font names<span class="val">trademark</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>I live and work in the United States, so this deck uses US law. I work up to the limit of what US law allows, and stay within it.</p>
      <p>The US Copyright Office treats typefaces as variations of letters, which cannot be owned. Font software is protected as a computer program. In Adobe v. Southern Software, the court found infringement in copied font software, not in the letterforms.</p>
      <p>This matters for training: a model learns from outlines, which are part of the font software, but it may output only letterforms. Whether the weights copy the software is not settled.</p>
      <p>Other countries differ. The UK and Germany give typeface designs some protection. Verify the details before presenting.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Open models" label="Open question: model licenses" planned>
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">02 Open models</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Open question</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">What license should a model trained on a font carry?</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Are the weights a Modified Version?</li>
          <li>Is shipping the model shipping the font?</li>
          <li>Does redrawing the font matter?</li>
          <li>What should Runebender recommend?</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>To be answered. Research notes: ~/Desktop/Model-License-Research.md.</p>
      <p>Cases to work through: a model trained only on the designer’s own fonts (Virtua Grotesk, which is OFL, and the Shapes font, which is Apache 2.0 or MIT); a model trained on another designer’s OFL font (Nasta’liq Distilled used Gulzar); a model trained on a commercial font under a EULA that forbids training.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── 03 Concepts ── -->

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Small models">
    <div class="sw sw-split">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">03 Concepts</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Small models</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Font models are small enough to train on a laptop.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>One to a few dozen megabytes</li>
          <li>Minutes to train on a laptop GPU</li>
          <li>Trained only on your own drawings</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <Fig class="sw-fig fig-right" src={nufoEditing} alt="Runebender editing Arabic glyphs with a rendered preview." />
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A PC with a good graphics card can train larger models. The font models are one to a few dozen megabytes. Training one from scratch takes minutes on a laptop GPU.</p>
      <p>Most font generation research trains on large collections of scraped fonts, which mix many drawing conventions. These models train on the designer’s own drawings, made on one grid.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Models are folders">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">03 Concepts</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Models are folders</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">A model is a folder of files on your disk.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li><code>~/Runebender/models/shapes</code></li>
          <li><code>~/Runebender/models/bolden</code></li>
          <li><code>~/Runebender/models/chat</code></li>
          <li><span><code>MyFont.models/</code>, beside the font</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A Runebender model folder holds a config, the weights, a vocabulary, and sometimes a manifest. A chat model folder holds a GGUF file.</p>
      <p>A font’s own models are in MyFont.models/ beside it, so they move with the font. The editor reads only these files. RUNEBENDER_MODELS sets a different root for one run.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="The training loop">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">03 Concepts</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">The training loop</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Your corrections become new training data.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Draw a core set and grade it green</li>
          <li>Train on the green set</li>
          <li>The model drafts the rest</li>
          <li>Correct the drafts and grade them</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Drafts come in batches of 10 to 20 glyphs on the 8-unit grid. Corrected drafts are graded green and join the training set, so each run needs fewer corrections.</p>
      <p>Build the font and proof it in text. Name each recurring error and add a check for it before the next run.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Drafts are proposals">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">03 Concepts</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Drafts are proposals</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">A model never edits the font directly.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>You install or discard each draft</li>
          <li>Only a person grades a glyph green</li>
          <li>Only you save the font</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Machine drafts stay orange until they are graded. Drafts land on the 8-unit grid. The designer makes the 2-unit optical corrections.</p>
      <p>Each engine is scored against drawn pairs. The baseline is a plain mean shift. The same rules apply to local models, to agents over MCP, and to the Chat panel.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

