      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">05 Hardware</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Memory bandwidth</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Faster memory gives faster answers.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Each token reads the active weights once</li>
          <li>Upper limit: bandwidth ÷ model size</li>
          <li>273 GB/s ÷ 17.6 GB: about 15 tokens/s</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The example is qwen3.6-27b on a MacBook Pro with an M4 Pro. Measured speed is lower than the limit.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Mixture of experts">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">05 Hardware</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Mixture of experts</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">The model reads only part of its weights for each token.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>35B parameters, 3B active</li>
          <li>The memory of a large model</li>
          <li>The speed of a small model</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
