      <p>qwen3.6-35b-a3b on the M4 Pro: upper limit about 130 tokens/s, against about 15 for the dense 27B.</p>
      <p>Measured on AMD Strix Halo, 128 GB: Qwen3 30B-A3B about 100 tokens/s, gpt-oss-120b about 55, dense Llama 3.3 70B about 5.</p>
      <p>Many current open models are mixtures of experts, including gpt-oss and the Qwen3 A3B models. This is why machines with large unified memory are useful for local inference.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Three kinds of machine">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">05 Hardware</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Three kinds of machine</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Each trades memory size against speed.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Graphics card<span class="val">24 to 32 GB, fastest</span></li>
          <li>Unified memory<span class="val">up to 512 GB</span></li>
          <li>Multiple cards<span class="val">labs and teams</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Graphics card: NVIDIA’s CUDA is supported by all inference and training tools.</p>
      <p>Unified memory: the processor and the graphics share one pool, with lower bandwidth than a card. Macs, AMD Strix Halo, NVIDIA DGX Spark.</p>
      <p>Multiple cards: tinybox, RTX Pro 6000 builds, DGX Station. Small models, such as the Runebender font models, also train on Apple Metal.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

