        <li>RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell: 96 GB per card, $9,000 to $16,000. tinybox green v2: 4 × RTX 5090, about $45,000.</li>
        <li>DGX Station GB300: 748 GB, 252 GB of it HBM at 7.1 TB/s, $85,000 to $160,000 from OEM partners.</li>
        <li>Mac Pro: discontinued March 2026, replaced by the Mac Studio.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Linux PC parts: 1000 W power supply for two cards, 64 GB system memory, 2 TB NVMe. llama.cpp splits a model across cards.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Choosing a machine">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">05 Hardware</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Choosing a machine</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Start with the computer you have.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Font models train on a laptop</li>
          <li>A 4B chat model needs 8 GB</li>
          <li>120B chat models need 128 GB</li>
          <li>When a task is too slow, buy memory</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <ul>
        <li>Train small font models: a Mac with Apple silicon, or an NVIDIA card with 8 GB or more.</li>
        <li>30B models at high speed: a 24 to 32 GB NVIDIA card, or a Mac with 48 GB or more.</li>
        <li>The largest open models: Mac Studio M5 Ultra, 256 to 512 GB.</li>
        <li>Teams, or training large models: RTX Pro 6000 builds, tinybox, DGX Station.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>My setup: MacBook Pro M4 Pro 48 GB for chat and coding with qwen3.6-35b-a3b. Linux PC with an RTX 2060 SUPER, 8 GB, for training, about 100 s per epoch. Steam Deck runs qwen3.5-4b at 18 tokens/s.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Runtimes">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">05 Hardware</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Runtimes</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">A runtime loads a model and runs it.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>llama.cpp<span class="val">chat models</span></li>
          <li>font-ml<span class="val">Runebender models</span></li>
          <li>MLX<span class="val">Apple</span></li>
          <li>Ollama, LM Studio<span class="val">desktop apps</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>llama.cpp runs GGUF files on Mac, NVIDIA, AMD, and CPU. font-ml uses Candle and trains as well as runs.</p>
      <p>Ollama and LM Studio are built on llama.cpp and MLX. They are the simplest first step outside Runebender. vLLM serves many users at once on NVIDIA servers.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── 06 Setup ── -->

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Installation" planned>
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">06 Setup</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Installation</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Two ways to install Runebender.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Mac: the DMG from runebender.org</li>
          <li>Mac and Linux: install with Cargo</li>
          <li><span>Check with <code>font-ml tasks</code></span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>cargo install --git https://github.com/fontgarden/runebender</p>
      <p>cargo install --git https://github.com/eliheuer/font-ml --features metal (or cuda, or no feature for CPU)</p>
      <p>Linux (Debian or Ubuntu) needs system libraries first: libfontconfig-dev libwayland-dev libxkbcommon-dev libx11-dev libxcb1-dev libvulkan1 libssl-dev libglib2.0-dev libgtk-3-dev libxdo-dev libasound2-dev.</p>
      <p>Compiling takes about fifteen minutes.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Setup with a coding agent">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">06 Setup</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Setup with a coding agent</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Give Claude Code or Codex this request.</p>
        <pre class="sw-code code">Read https://runebender.org/
local-ai-setup-prompt.md
and set up Runebender for
local AI on this machine.</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The instructions tell the agent to inspect the machine first, ask before each download or install, leave your fonts unchanged, train a first shapes model, and report what it did.</p>
      <p>The chat model download is optional, about 800 MB. The agent does not open the editor unless asked.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Exercises">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">06 Setup</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Exercises</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Work through the demo repository in order.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Draw from text</li>
          <li>Train and bolden</li>
          <li>Chat model</li>
          <li>Node editor</li>
          <li>A real font</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>git clone https://github.com/eliheuer/runebender-local-ai-demo</p>
      <ul>
        <li>01-draw-from-text: train a shapes model from scratch, then fine-tune it.</li>
        <li>02-train-and-bolden: train a bolden model and bolden a new shape.</li>
        <li>03-chat-model: download an open-weight chat model and ask about the font.</li>
        <li>04-nodes: the same steps as a workflow in the node editor.</li>
        <li>05-real-font: the bolden loop on Virtua Grotesk.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Exercise 01 is the one to finish if there is time for only one. To reset the fonts, run git checkout -- fonts.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── 07 Agents ── -->

  <Slide section="Agents" label="Agents over MCP">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">07 Agents</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Agents over MCP</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Coding agents can work in the open editor.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li><code>runebender mcp --install</code></li>
          <li>47 tools and 6 workflows</li>
          <li>They propose, you install</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Works with Claude Code, Codex, Claude Desktop, and Cursor. The agent works on the open document, including unsaved changes. It reads first and proofs the result.</p>
      <p>Workflows: review-glyph, triage-font, compatibility-check, spacing-pass, kerning-pass, draft-weight.</p>
      <p>Figma and Penpot have MCP servers, Glyphs has a third-party one, and FontLab 9 has an in-app chat. Runebender adds a review step and a safety contract: proposals, receipts, no saves.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Agents" label="Planned work">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">07 Agents</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Planned work</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">The same rules apply to each new feature.</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>Spacing and kerning drafts</li>
          <li>Drawing a missing character</li>
          <li>A model that ranks drafts</li>
          <li>A Proof panel in the editor</li>
          <li>One model for several families</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Drafts are proposals, only the designer grades, and every engine is scored against drawn pairs.</p>
      <p>The ranking model is scored against the designer’s corrections before it is used. The second family for the shared model is Virtua Oldstyle, drawn on the same grid.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="End" label="Links">
    <div class="sw">
      <div class="sw-head"><p class="sw-sec">Links</p><p class="sw-talk">{TALK}</p><p class="sw-num"></p></div>
      <h2 class="sw-title t-display">Thank you</h2>
      <div class="sw-body">
        <p class="sw-lead t-head">Eli Heuer<br />eli@elih.net</p>
        <ol class="sw-points">
          <li>runebender.org</li>
          <li>github.com/fontgarden/runebender</li>
          <li>github.com/eliheuer/font-ml</li>
